export const lessonTeaching={
compound:[['What compounding means','A return earned in one period can remain invested and participate in later returns. Contributions add principal; investment returns are variable and can be negative. A smooth calculator line hides the uneven path investors experience.'],['Compare scenarios carefully','Change one assumption at a time: contribution amount, duration or hypothetical rate. The result is a mathematical illustration, not a forecast. Fees, taxes, inflation and changing returns affect real outcomes.']],
diversify:[['Why diversification matters','Holding different companies, sectors and asset types can reduce the effect of a single holding performing badly. Correlations can rise during stress, so diversification does not eliminate market loss.'],['Look through the labels','Several funds may own the same large companies. Examine underlying holdings, fees, geography and concentration rather than counting fund names.']],
account:[['A practical account-opening sequence','First identify the goal and time horizon; compare taxable brokerage, Roth IRA and traditional IRA rules; verify provider fees and investment access; then use the provider’s official application. Expect identity verification. This app does not collect it.'],['Fund, then invest','Choose a funding method and, for an IRA contribution, the correct tax year. Deposited cash may remain in a settlement or core position. Choose investments separately and review the order and risks.']],
ira:[['Roth versus traditional in context','Roth IRA contributions are generally after-tax; qualified distributions can be tax-free when requirements are met. Traditional contributions may be deductible depending on circumstances, while distributions are generally taxable. A personal tax result depends on the rules and facts that apply to you.'],['The 2026 limit snapshot','For 2026, the general IRA contribution limit is $7,500, plus a $1,100 catch-up contribution at age 50 or older, subject to eligible compensation and other rules. Roth and traditional contributions share that combined limit. Always check IRS guidance for the tax year.']],
budget:[['Make irregular costs visible','Tuition timing, textbooks, travel and annual fees can make a monthly plan look better than reality. Convert predictable irregular costs into a monthly set-aside. Keep estimates separate from confirmed bills.'],['Choose a useful first buffer','An accessible cash reserve can help absorb surprises. A reasonable target depends on expenses, income stability and obligations; start with a manageable step and revisit it.']],
macro:[['Rates and inflation affect different businesses differently','Higher rates can raise borrowing costs and change the present value investors assign to future cash flows. Inflation can raise a company’s input costs and selling prices at different speeds. The net effect depends on the business and expectations.'],['Read a release, not just a headline','Check the data period, revisions, definitions and comparison with expectations. One report rarely establishes a trend. Separate what the source measured from the interpretation in a headline.']],
thesis:[['A testable research thesis','Describe how the company earns revenue, the drivers of future cash flows, the current valuation question, and evidence that would weaken your view. Keep observations distinct from assumptions.'],['Check primary evidence','Use annual and quarterly filings, earnings releases and financial statements. Study risk disclosures, debt, cash flow, segment trends and share dilution. A popular company can still be an unsuitable or overpriced investment.']],
statements:[['How the statements connect','The income statement records revenue and expenses over a period; the balance sheet shows assets, liabilities and equity at a point in time; the cash-flow statement reconciles cash movement. Net income is not the same as cash generated.'],['Follow one change through','Higher depreciation lowers reported earnings but is added back in operating cash flow; cash may still change through working capital or capital spending. Read the statements together and check the notes.']],
accounts:[['Mutual funds and ETFs','These investment vehicles pool holdings. An index fund tracks a defined index; an actively managed fund follows its stated strategy. Funds can diversify but still lose money. Compare expense ratios, holdings, tracking and trading details.'],['Stocks and bonds','A stock represents an ownership claim; a bond is a debt claim with issuer and interest-rate risks. Neither guarantees a profit. Diversification, time horizon and liquidity needs shape how investors evaluate risk.']]
};
